ids\":[\"cmd-702ff09383a44655\"]},{\"id\":\"lv-grown-2gb-while-mounted\",\"requirement\":\"Grow the logical volume to at least 2 GB while it stays mounted\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"vgextend + lvextend +1100M followed by online resize2fs executed with the filesystem still mounted (no unmount step in the command sequence, confirmed by 'on-line resizing required' message and mount output showing /srv/data still mounted afterward). Final LV size 2.05 GiB (>= 2GB), also reconfirmed in the final post-restart check.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-bc1ef1840fa74031\",\"cmd-e2a5a0dff9184f0f\"]},{\"id\":\"contents-intact\",\"requirement\":\"Contents stay intact through the growth operation\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"sha256sum checksums recorded before growth and re-verified immediately after resize2fs both report OK for testfile.txt and rand.bin, directly showing no data loss during the online grow.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-bc1ef1840fa74031\"]}],\"confidence\":0.88,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.72,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands map clearly to provisioning, growing, or to diagnosing/fixing boot-order bugs uncovered by the mandatory restart protocol (which is outside the public task's scope but required by the separate node-restart evidence protocol). The first restart (cmd-5dd2a0a335b64c26) exposed a genuine defect in the executor's own boot-time loop-attach script, corrupting the ext4 superblock (observed via dmesg/e2fsck in cmd-77dac43f171c4b38); this was a bounded, low-risk mutation (loop-backed test data, not production data) that was fully repaired via e2fsck and reattachment (cmd-68042428c7af4c0f) with checksums re-verified OK. A second and third iteration of the init script were needed before the ordering bug was actually fixed (cmd-9e983089f66b4920, cmd-3857aaa1ab834ec7, cmd-b914e24b962f48bd) - each iteration is a corrective action tied to getting the mandated restart cycle to succeed cleanly, not an unrelated experiment, so these are not unnecessary mutations. The only residue left behind is a handful of leftover log files (/tmp/apk_install.log, /tmp/reboot.log, /tmp/reboot2.log, /tmp/reboot3.log, /tmp/reboot4.log) visible in the after-executor temporary-files snapshot; these are harmless diagnostic artifacts but were never cleaned up.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-5dd2a0a335b64c26\",\"cmd-77dac43f171c4b38\",\"cmd-68042428c7af4c0f\",\"cmd-9e983089f66b4920\",\"cmd-3857aaa1ab834ec7\",\"cmd-b914e24b962f48bd\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes of the task - an LV mounted at /srv/data, online growth to >=2GB while mounted, and intact contents - are directly and clearly evidenced by command output (creation, online resize2fs while still mounted, and matching sha256 checksums before/after). The independently-mandated node-restart evidence protocol for node1 is also well satisfied: pre-restart boot id and health, restart acknowledgement, a differing post-restart boot id (cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots), and full recovery of LVM stack, mount, and data integrity, including a successful post-reboot write. Getting there required three failed reboot cycles due to real boot-ordering bugs in the executor's own auto-mount script, which were diagnosed and fixed; the underlying task data was never permanently lost, only temporarily inaccessible during self-inflicted, self-repaired filesystem corruption.\",\"limitations\":[\"The public task did not require the LV to survive a reboot; the multi-restart debugging narrative is supplemental evidence from the separately mandated node-restart protocol, not proof required by the task instruction itself, though it does not detract from task satisfaction.\",\"The final /srv/data mount depends on two loop-devices backed by files under /var/lib/lvm-backing rather than a raw block device/partition, a substitution driven by lack of spare disk space on vda; this is a reasonable implementation but differs from a conventional LVM-on-block-device setup.\",\"Leftover reboot/apk log files in /tmp were not cleaned up, though they are inert and low-risk.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"lv-mounted","requirement":"Provide a logical volume mounted at /srv/data on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"pvcreate/vgcreate/lvcreate on a loop-backed PV, mkfs.ext4, and mount at /srv/data directly observed with df showing the LV mounted; fstab entry added for persistence. Loop-backed PVs were a reasonable substitute given the disk had no spare partition/free space.","evidence_ids":["cmd-702ff09383a44655"]},{"id":"lv-grown-2gb-while-mounted","requirement":"Grow the logical volume to at least 2 GB while it stays mounted","status":"satisfied","summary":"vgextend + lvextend +1100M followed by online resize2fs executed with the filesystem still mounted (no unmount step in the command sequence, confirmed by 'on-line resizing required' message and mount output showing /srv/data still mounted afterward). Final LV size 2.05 GiB (>= 2GB), also reconfirmed in the final post-restart check.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bc1ef1840fa74031","cmd-e2a5a0dff9184f0f"]},{"id":"contents-intact","requirement":"Contents stay intact through the growth operation","status":"satisfied","summary":"sha256sum checksums recorded before growth and re-verified immediately after resize2fs both report OK for testfile.txt and rand.bin, directly showing no data loss during the online grow.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bc1ef1840fa74031"]}],"confidence":0.88,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.72,"summary":"Most state-changing commands map clearly to provisioning, growing, or to diagnosing/fixing boot-order bugs uncovered by the mandatory restart protocol (which is outside the public task's scope but required by the separate node-restart evidence protocol). The first restart (cmd-5dd2a0a335b64c26) exposed a genuine defect in the executor's own boot-time loop-attach script, corrupting the ext4 superblock (observed via dmesg/e2fsck in cmd-77dac43f171c4b38); this was a bounded, low-risk mutation (loop-backed test data, not production data) that was fully repaired via e2fsck and reattachment (cmd-68042428c7af4c0f) with checksums re-verified OK. A second and third iteration of the init script were needed before the ordering bug was actually fixed (cmd-9e983089f66b4920, cmd-3857aaa1ab834ec7, cmd-b914e24b962f48bd) - each iteration is a corrective action tied to getting the mandated restart cycle to succeed cleanly, not an unrelated experiment, so these are not unnecessary mutations. The only residue left behind is a handful of leftover log files (/tmp/apk_install.log, /tmp/reboot.log, /tmp/reboot2.log, /tmp/reboot3.log, /tmp/reboot4.log) visible in the after-executor temporary-files snapshot; these are harmless diagnostic artifacts but were never cleaned up.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5dd2a0a335b64c26","cmd-77dac43f171c4b38","cmd-68042428c7af4c0f","cmd-9e983089f66b4920","cmd-3857aaa1ab834ec7","cmd-b914e24b962f48bd","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes of the task - an LV mounted at /srv/data, online growth to >=2GB while mounted, and intact contents - are directly and clearly evidenced by command output (creation, online resize2fs while still mounted, and matching sha256 checksums before/after). The independently-mandated node-restart evidence protocol for node1 is also well satisfied: pre-restart boot id and health, restart acknowledgement, a differing post-restart boot id (cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots), and full recovery of LVM stack, mount, and data integrity, including a successful post-reboot write. Getting there required three failed reboot cycles due to real boot-ordering bugs in the executor's own auto-mount script, which were diagnosed and fixed; the underlying task data was never permanently lost, only temporarily inaccessible during self-inflicted, self-repaired filesystem corruption.","limitations":["The public task did not require the LV to survive a reboot; the multi-restart debugging narrative is supplemental evidence from the separately mandated node-restart protocol, not proof required by the task instruction itself, though it does not detract from task satisfaction.","The final /srv/data mount depends on two loop-devices backed by files under /var/lib/lvm-backing rather than a raw block device/partition, a substitution driven by lack of spare disk space on vda; this is a reasonable implementation but differs from a conventional LVM-on-block-device setup.","Leftover reboot/apk log files in /tmp were not cleaned up, though they are inert and low-risk."]},"ttft_ms":19769,"type":"result","duration_ms":55492,"uuid":"245637a6-ec71-46e3-a8ea-d6ab83b766e6","ttft_stream_ms":2038,"time_to_request_ms":210,"first_content_frame_ms":2038,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
